= Solution
{parent=a}

One general form of the <Erdős-Kac theorem> is the following. Let $f$ be a real <strongly additive arithmetic function>, with $|f(p)|\le C$ for all <primes>, and define
$$
A(x)=\sum_{p\le x}\frac{f(p)}p,\qquad B(x)^2=\sum_{p\le x}\frac{f(p)^2}p.
$$
If $B(x)\to\infty$, then under the <discrete uniform distribution> on $[N]$,
$$
\boxed{\frac{f(n)-A(N)}{B(N)}\ \xrightarrow{\mathrm d}\ \mathcal N(0,1).}
$$
Here the arrow denotes <convergence in distribution> and $\mathcal N(0,1)$ is the <standard normal distribution>. Equivalently, the probability of being at most $z$ tends to the <standard normal distribution function> $\Phi(z)$ for every real $z$. Replacing $B(x)^2$ by $\sum_{p\le x}f(p)^2(1-1/p)/p$ is equivalent, because the difference is bounded by $C^2\sum_p p^{-2}$.

This is a general bounded-prime-weight form, not just the special case $f=\omega$. In that case the <Mertens theorem for reciprocal primes> gives $A(N)=\log\log N+O(1)$ and $B(N)^2=\log\log N+O(1)$, so **the classical prime-factor central limit theorem** is
$$
\boxed{\frac{\omega(n)-\log\log N}{\sqrt{\log\log N}}\ \xrightarrow{\mathrm d}\ \mathcal N(0,1).}
$$

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $y=N^{1/\varphi(N)}$, $L=\sum_{p\le y}|g(p)|$, and $A=\sum_{p\le y}g(p)/p$. Put $I_p(n)=\mathbf1_{p\mid n}$ and $Y_p=I_p-1/p$. The <strongly additive arithmetic function> identity is $g-A=\sum_{p\le y}g(p)Y_p$.

For any tuple $p_1,\ldots,p_j$, expand $\prod_{r=1}^j(I_{p_r}-1/p_r)$ by choosing an indicator in some positions and the constant in the others. If the selected positions contain distinct <primes> whose product is $d$, then
$$
\mathbb E_N\prod_{r\text{ selected}}I_{p_r}=\frac{\lfloor N/d\rfloor}N=\frac1d+O(N^{-1}).
$$
For the corresponding product of the independent <Bernoulli random variables> $X_p$, the <expectation> is exactly $1/d$. This includes repeated <primes>, since their <indicator functions> are idempotent, and the empty selection has zero error. Summing absolute values of all expansion coefficients bounds the difference by
$$
\frac1N\prod_{r=1}^j\left(1+\frac1{p_r}\right)\le\frac{2^j}{N}\le\frac{y^j}{N}
$$
when $y\ge2$. Expanding the $j$th power and summing the absolute coefficient weights gives
$$
\mathbb E_N(g-A)^j=\mathbb E\left(\sum_{p\le y}g(p)(X_p-1/p)\right)^j+O\left(\frac{y^jL^j}{N}\right).
$$

It remains to center at the actual <expectation>. Since $\mathbb E_N I_p=\lfloor N/p\rfloor/N$, we have $|\mathbb E_Ng-A|\le L/N$. For each $n$, both $g(n)-A$ and $g(n)-\mathbb E_Ng$ have absolute value at most $L$, because all their indicator coefficients have absolute value at most one. The identity $u^j-v^j=(u-v)\sum_{r=0}^{j-1}u^{j-1-r}v^r$ therefore bounds the change in the <central moment> by $jL^j/N$.

Finally, <independence> factors each term of the independent <central moment>. If $q_1,\ldots,q_w$ are the distinct <primes> of the tuple and $a_i$ their multiplicities, **the desired comparison** becomes
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E_N(g-\mathbb E_Ng)^j=\sum_{p_1,\ldots,p_j\le y}\prod_{i=1}^w g(q_i)^{a_i}\mathbb E(X_{q_i}-1/q_i)^{a_i}+O\left(\frac{j y^j}{N}L^j\right).}
$$
If $y<2$, then $g=0$ and both sides vanish, so the formula remains valid. The argument also covers complex coefficients $g(p)$; a real-valued assumption is needed for the later <normal distribution> conclusion, not for this <centered moment comparison for additive arithmetic functions>.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A useful <method of moments in probability> states that if real <random variables> $Z_N$ have all moments, $\mathbb E Z_N^j\to m_j$ for every integer $j\ge1$, and $(m_j)$ is the moment sequence of a <moment-determinate probability distribution>, then $Z_N$ converges to that distribution in the sense of <convergence in distribution>. In particular, convergence to the <standard normal distribution> follows from limiting odd moments zero and limiting even moments $(2k-1)!!$. The <moment-generating function of a standard normal variable>, finite in a neighbourhood of zero, ensures <moment determinacy>.

For the classical <Erdős-Kac theorem>, put $L=\log\log N$ and choose, for example, $\varphi(N)=L^{1/4}$, with a harmless modification for small $N$. Truncate the <prime omega function> to <primes> $p\le y=N^{1/\varphi(N)}$. The omitted number of distinct <prime factors> of any $n\le N$ is at most $\varphi(N)$, since a product of $r$ omitted <primes> exceeds $N^{r/\varphi(N)}$. Thus this truncation changes the normalized variable by $o(1)$ uniformly. Also, the <Mertens theorem for reciprocal primes> gives
$$
\sum_{p\le y}\frac1p=L-\log\varphi(N)+O(1)=L+o(\sqrt L).
$$

Part (b) transfers every fixed normalized <central moment> of the truncated <prime omega function> to the independent <Bernoulli random variables> model. Indeed, its unnormalized error is at most $O_j(N^{2j/\varphi(N)-1})$, using $\sum_{p\le y}1\le y$, and this tends to zero for each fixed $j$. In the independent expansion, any singleton index has zero <expectation>; the leading contributions are pairings, giving exactly the <standard normal distribution> moments, while blocks of size at least three are negligible after normalization. The <method of moments in probability>, followed by the uniformly negligible truncation and centering errors, proves the <Erdős-Kac theorem>.

The same strategy proves the bounded-prime-weight form in part (a). Choose $\varphi(N)\to\infty$ slowly enough that $\varphi(N)=o(B(N))$. The omitted weighted contribution is $O(\varphi(N))$; its mean is likewise $O(\varphi(N))$, and its independent <variance> is $O(\log\varphi(N)+1)=o(B(N)^2)$ by the <Mertens theorem for reciprocal primes>. Hence the truncated model has the same normalization, and part (b) again transfers each fixed <central moment>.

= d
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $L=\log\log N$, $h=L^{-1/2}$, and let $F_N$ be the <cumulative distribution function> of $(\omega-L)/\sqrt L$. Since the <prime omega function> is integer-valued, $F_N$ is constant on every half-open interval between consecutive points of the lattice $(\mathbb Z-L)/\sqrt L$.

Choose $k=\lfloor L\rfloor$ and put $\alpha=(k-L)/\sqrt L$, $\beta=(k+1-L)/\sqrt L$. Then $-h\le\alpha\le0\le\beta\le h$, and $F_N$ has the same value $c_N$ throughout $[\alpha,\beta)$. The <standard normal distribution function> $\Phi$ is continuous, so the definition of the <Kolmogorov distance> gives
$$
E(N)\ge|c_N-\Phi(\alpha)|,\qquad E(N)\ge|c_N-\Phi(\beta)|,
$$
where the second inequality follows by taking a limit from below at $\beta$. The <triangle inequality> therefore yields
$$
2E(N)\ge\Phi(\beta)-\Phi(\alpha)=\int_\alpha^\beta\frac{e^{-t^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}\,dt\ge\frac{h e^{-h^2/2}}{\sqrt{2\pi}}.
$$
Thus **the integer lattice forces the lower bound**:
$$
\boxed{E(N)\ge\frac{e^{-1/(2\log\log N)}}{2\sqrt{2\pi\log\log N}}\gg\frac1{\sqrt{\log\log N}}.}
$$
This <lattice obstruction to normal approximation> does not require a quantitative <Erdős-Kac theorem>; it follows directly from discreteness and the positive <standard normal density> near zero.
